5d:T11ed,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"Higher Study Application Guide — 18-Step Journey","description":"Complete 18-step application journey for studying abroad, with required documents checklist, 12-month timeline and common mistakes to avoid.","datePublished":"2025-01-01","dateModified":"2026-09-30","inLanguage":"en","author":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Alaminn.info Higher Study","url":"https://alaminn.info"},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Alaminn.info","url":"https://alaminn.info","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://alaminn.info/logo.svg"}},"mainEntityOfPage":{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://alaminn.info/higher-study/application-guide"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Higher Study","item":"https://alaminn.info/higher-study"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Application Guide","item":"https://alaminn.info/higher-study/application-guide"}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"When should I start preparing my application?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For September intake (most common in the UK, USA, Canada), start 12 months ahead — around September of the year before. For January/May intakes (Australia, some UK/US universities), start 8–9 months ahead. PhD applications often need 12–18 months because of supervisor identification."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How many universities should I apply to?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"We recommend 6–8: 2 reach (above your profile), 3–4 match (in line with your profile), 1–2 safety (well within your profile). For UCAS undergrad in the UK, you can apply to a maximum of 5 universities on a single application."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need to take an English test if I studied in English?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It depends. Some universities waive the English requirement if your previous degree was taught in English (typically in the UK, USA, Ireland, Australia, New Zealand, Canada — but not all). Check each university's English waiver policy. If you're from Bangladesh, India, Pakistan or Nigeria, you usually still need to take an English test."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What's the difference between SOP and personal statement?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"An SOP (Statement of Purpose) is typically longer (500–1000 words) and focuses on academic + research interests + career goals — used for postgraduate applications. A personal statement (UCAS undergrad in the UK) is shorter (4000 characters max) and broader — about why you want to study the subject, what makes you suitable, and your wider interests."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I apply for scholarships before I have an offer?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes — and you should! Many scholarships (Chevening, Commonwealth, Erasmus Mundus, DAAD) have deadlines BEFORE course application deadlines, or run in parallel. Apply for scholarships as soon as applications open — usually 10–12 months before course start."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much bank balance do I need to show for a student visa?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It varies by country. UK: tuition for first year + £1,334/month (London) or £1,023/month (outside London) for 9 months. USA: cost shown on I-20. Australia: AUD 24,505/year + travel + tuition. Canada: CAD 10,000/year (Quebec: CAD 11,411) + tuition. Funds must usually be held for 28+ consecutive days before applying."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What happens if my visa is refused?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You can usually re-apply, but you'll lose time and may need to defer your course start date. Common refusal reasons: insufficient financial evidence, weak English score, inconsistent SOP, or failing the credibility interview. Use our consultation service to review your visa application before submitting — it's free."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Should I apply through an agent or directly?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Both routes work. Direct applications give you full control. Applying through a verified partner like ALO Education (via our consent-tracked referral) gives you document review, deadline tracking and often application fee waivers — without surrendering control of your data. Always check an agent's credentials and never pay for 'guaranteed admission'."}}]}]}23:["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"$5d"}}]
